Carpal Tunnel Syndrome
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Paresthesia in median nerve distribution, Nocturnal symptom exacerbation, Positive Tinel/Phalen signs, Thenar atrophy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-011
Reason for Visit: Acute nocturnal exacerbation of severe hand pain and numbness preventing sleep and functional use of the dominant hand.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated severe, sleep-depriving upper limb pain and sudden weakness causing the patient to drop objects. Urgent assessment was required to evaluate for acute compressive neuropathy versus an acute central vascular event, and to initiate mechanical offloading and analgesia to prevent further nerve ischemia and restore sleep capability prior to daytime services.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My hand is completely numb and throbbing so badly it woke me up, and I can't even hold my water glass."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with a severe acute-on-chronic exacerbation of right hand symptoms. Timeline: Intermittent numbness and tingling localized to the thumb, index, and middle fingers for 3 months; progressed over the last week to clumsiness and dropping of objects. Tonight, the patient was awakened by severe, throbbing pain radiating proximally into the volar forearm, accompanied by profound paresthesia. Patient reports temporary, partial relief achieved only by forcefully shaking or wringing the hands (positive flick sign).
Functional Status: Impaired fine motor function of the dominant hand; unable to perform basic grasping tasks.
Social/Safety Concerns: Sleep deprivation and functional limitation of the dominant hand affecting independent self-care.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/86 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Alert, fatigued, continuously shaking the right hand and rubbing the wrist.
Physical Exam: Inspection reveals early, mild atrophy of the right thenar eminence musculature compared to the left. Sensory testing demonstrates decreased light touch and two-point discrimination (>5mm) strictly in the median nerve distribution (palmar thumb, index, middle, and radial half of ring finger). Sensation over the thenar eminence itself is preserved (palmar cutaneous branch sparing). Motor testing reveals 4/5 weakness in thumb abduction against resistance. Positive Tinel's sign with paresthesia radiating into the fingers upon tapping the volar wrist. Positive Phalen's maneuver reproducing exact symptoms within 30 seconds of sustained passive wrist flexion. Radial and ulnar pulses intact.
Environmental Observations: Patient’s workstation (laptop and poorly designed mouse setup) visible in the adjacent room, suggesting an occupational mechanism.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Took Paracetamol 1g two hours ago with nil effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute exacerbation of Carpal Tunnel Syndrome - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Cervical Radiculopathy (C6/C7) - Ruled out clinically by the absence of neck pain, negative Spurling's maneuver, and intact brachioradialis/triceps reflexes.

Transient Ischemic Attack (TIA) - Ruled out by the strictly peripheral, single-nerve distribution of symptoms, absence of facial droop, normal speech, and classical mechanical provocation signs.

Pronator Teres Syndrome - Ruled out by the absence of proximal forearm aching on resisted pronation and the distinct presence of nocturnal awakening (a hallmark of wrist-level compression).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with classic features of median nerve compression at the wrist, experiencing an acute, sleep-preventing pain crisis. The profound nocturnal exacerbation, positive provocative testing (Tinel's/Phalen's), and focal neurological deficits localized the pathology exclusively to the carpal tunnel, safely excluding acute central neurovascular events. The severity of the pain and functional weakness, resulting in an inability to sleep or perform basic grip functions, created an acute distress state. Delaying intervention until regular clinic hours would have subjected the patient to significant ongoing nerve ischemia and prolonged suffering. Urgent after-hours assessment provided diagnostic clarity, ruled out life-threatening alternatives, and facilitated the immediate application of mechanical splinting to decompress the nerve.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Ibuprofen 400mg TDS for 5 days to reduce flexor tenosynovitis.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Wear the provided rigid wrist splint strictly every night while sleeping. Consequence: Prevention of involuntary wrist flexion, keeping the tunnel open and preventing the agonizing nerve compression that causes night waking (negative reinforcement).
Action: Perform gentle median nerve gliding exercises every 2 hours during the day. Consequence: Improved blood flow and reduced tethering of the nerve, resulting in noticeably less tingling and heaviness during functional tasks (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A
DME/Safety (Optional): Fitted a neutral-position volar wrist splint.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 1-2 weeks for formal nerve conduction studies and possible corticosteroid injection or surgical referral if conservative measures fail.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if there is a sudden, complete loss of motor function in the hand, or if new symptoms of facial droop, slurred speech, or whole-arm weakness develop.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; nerve conduction studies are required to quantify the exact severity of the nerve damage. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The splinting will often provide immediate relief from nocturnal pain, but the underlying structural narrowing requires ongoing management and potential surgical release to prevent permanent muscle wasting.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency care if you suddenly cannot move your hand at all, or if you develop numbness in your face or difficulty speaking.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The median nerve is being crushed in a tight tunnel at your wrist, especially when you sleep with your wrists curled. The splint physically holds the tunnel open, giving the nerve space to breathe and stopping the pain signals.
Actionable Steps: The splint must go on the moment you get into bed. Do not try to sleep without it. This simple mechanical block is the most effective way to stop the nerve from dying.

